WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.460
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:09.410
This paper is about endowing colored images
with depth information, which is typically

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.520
done through depth maps.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.410
Depth maps describe how far parts of the scene
are from the camera and are given with a color

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:22.349
coding where the darker the colors are, the
further away the objects are.

00:00:22.349 --> 00:00:27.710
These depth maps can be used to apply a variety
of effects to the image that require knowledge

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:32.580
about the distance of the objects within - for
instance, selectively defocusing parts of

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:38.500
the image, or even removing people and inserting
new objects to the scene.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:43.450
If we, humans look at an image, we have an
intuitive understanding of its contents and

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:47.390
have the knowledge to produce a depth map
by pen and paper.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:52.760
However, this would, of course, be infeasible
and would take too long, so we would prefer

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.410
a machine to do it for us instead.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:00.280
But of course, machines don’t understand
the concept of 3D geometry so they probably

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.280
cannot help us with this.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.140
Or, with the power of machine learning algorithms,
can they?

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:12.770
This new paper from scientists at Google Research
attempts to perform this, but, with a twist.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:18.950
The twist is that a learning algorithm is
unleashed on a dataset of what they call mannequins,

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:24.670
or in other words, real humans are asked to
stand around frozen in a variety of different

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:28.460
positions while the camera moves around in
the scene.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.740
The goal is that the algorithm would have
a look at these frozen people and take into

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:36.380
consideration the parallax of the camera movement.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:40.979
This means that the objects closer to the
camera move more than other objects that are

00:01:40.979 --> 00:01:42.229
further away.

00:01:42.229 --> 00:01:47.760
And turns out, this kind of knowledge can
be exploited, so much so that if we train

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:54.130
our AI properly, it will be able to predict
the depth maps of people that are moving around,

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:57.590
even if it had only seen these frozen mannequins
before.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:02.350
This is particularly difficult because if
we have an animation, we have to make sure

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:07.689
that the guesses are consistent across time,
or else we get these annoying flickering effects

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:09.830
that you see here with previous techniques.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:15.090
It is still there with the new method, especially
for the background, but the improvement on

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.200
the human part of the image is truly remarkable.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.790
Beyond the removal and insertion techniques
we talked about earlier, I am also really

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:28.290
excited for this method as it may open up
the possibility of creating video versions

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:32.810
of these amazing portrait mode images with
many of the newer smartphones people have

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.010
in their pockets.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.129
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

